{{also|Bronze|bronzé}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2010|February|21}}

===Etymology===
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Gladiator Bronzemedaillon.jpg|thumb|A bronze medallion]]
From {{bor|en|fr|bronze}} (1511), from {{der|en|it|bronzo}} (13th cent.), of {{unc|en|title=uncertain}} origin; see it for more. First use appears c. 1721 in the writings of {{w|Matthew Prior}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɹɒnz/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/bɹɑnz/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-bronze.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-bronze.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bronze.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒnz|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A naturally occurring or man-made [[alloy]] of [[copper]], usually in combination with [[tin]], but also with one or more other [[metal]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=|title=Poems on Several Occasions|author=Matthew Prior|page=339|passage=How little gives thee joy or pain; A print, a '''bronze''', a flower, a root.}}
#: {{cot|en|brass#Etymology_1}}
# {{lb|en|countable|and|uncountable}} A [[reddish]]-[[brown]] [[colour]], the colour of bronze.
#: {{color panel|826644}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[work of art]] made of bronze, especially a [[sculpture]].
# A [[bronze medal]].
#: {{ux|en|She wanted to win the tournament, but had to settle for the '''bronze''' after being beaten in the semi-finals.}}
# Boldness; [[impudence]].
#: {{syn|en|brass#Etymology_1}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Dunciad|passage=Embrown'd with native '''bronze''', lo! Henley stands.}}

